    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Soil pH between 5.5 and 7.0 is a crucial zone for grapevines, as it governs the solubility of essential micronutrients. In this slightly acidic to neutral range, the availability of iron ($\\text{Fe}$) and manganese ($\\text{Mn}$) is generally optimized, which is vital for preventing chlorosis in the vines. If the pH drifts too high, like above 7.5, these micronutrients become chemically 'locked up' in the soil, making them unavailable to the roots, even if they are physically present. This is because higher pH favors the formation of insoluble hydroxides of $\\text{Fe}$ and $\\text{Mn}$.",
