      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The chemical mechanisms are rooted in the equilibrium reactions that govern metal solubility, which is profoundly influenced by the concentration of hydrogen ions ($\\text{H}^+$). When the $\\text{pH}$ is in the 5.5 to 7.0 range, the concentration of $\\text{H}^+$ is sufficient to keep the $\\text{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$ ions in their soluble, bioavailable forms. As the $\\text{pH}$ rises (meaning $\\text{H}^+$ concentration drops), the equilibrium shifts towards the precipitation of these metals as less soluble hydroxides, such as $\\text{Fe}(\\text{OH})_3$ or $\\text{Mn}(\\text{OH})_2$. This process is a classic example of hydroxide precipitation driven by increasing alkalinity.",
